From 0215b9a4a1819eba267ab132095aefef5e7c48ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Edvinas=20Stun=C5=BE=C4=97nas?= <edvinas@8devices.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 20:56:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: fix the GI column order in the per-station
 tx_stats
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The tx_stats GI histogram is labelled

	GI %s (0.4us,0.8us,1.6us,3.2us)

but the index it is written at is

	gi = FIELD_GET(RATE_INFO_FLAGS_SHORT_GI, arsta->txrate.flags);

which is a single bit: 0 when the short-GI flag is clear, 1 when it is
set. Bucket 0 is therefore 0.8 us and bucket 1 is 0.4 us -- the label has
the first two columns the wrong way round, so every reading of it is
inverted. The same file already prints the pair in the correct order for
the HTT peer stats a few lines further down:

	"GI: 0.8us %llu 0.4us %llu 1.6us %llu 3.2us %llu\n"

This is not academic. A station honouring disable_sgi puts all of its
frames in bucket 0 and reads back as if it were transmitting nothing but
short GI, which is exactly the opposite of what happened.

Swap the first two column names. Note that buckets 2 and 3 are never
written at all, since the index is derived from one bit; they are left
in place rather than removing columns from an established debugfs
format.

ref: 8devices/software/yocto/meta-8dev-qcs405#713

Signed-off-by: Edvinas Stunžėnas <edvinas@8devices.com>
Upstream-Status: Pending

---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/debugfs_sta.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/debugfs_sta.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/debugfs_sta.c
index 168879a380cb..87984afd6f28 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/debugfs_sta.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/debugfs_sta.c
@@ -198,7 +198,7 @@ static ssize_t ath11k_dbg_sta_dump_tx_stats(struct file *file,
 					 stats->nss[j][0], stats->nss[j][1],
 					 stats->nss[j][2], stats->nss[j][3]);
 			len += scnprintf(buf + len, size - len,
-					 " GI %s (0.4us,0.8us,1.6us,3.2us)\n",
+					 " GI %s (0.8us,0.4us,1.6us,3.2us)\n",
 					 str[j]);
 			len += scnprintf(buf + len, size - len,
 					 "  %llu %llu %llu %llu\n",
